energy powered\n\nAnd also config_level characteristics.\n\nFor config_level, I could do one entry per attribute with axis \"meter_size_per_bore_and_power\" and conditions like \"reduced_bore, ac_mains, DN40-DN65\". Hmm, but each table has multiple rows with grouped meter sizes.\n\nAlternative approach: config_level with axis \"bore_and_power_source\" and conditions... no, the varying dimension within a table is meter size.\n\nLet me think about what's most useful. The values that vary:\n- indicating_range_max: 9999999 (all reduced/AC and most), 99999999 (large sizes), 999999999 (largest sizes)\n- indicating_range_min: 99999, 999999, 9999999, 99999999\n- verification_scale_interval_max: 0.0001 to 1\n\nThe grouping of meter sizes differs per table. So a per-table config_level entry with axis \"meter_size\" makes sense, with the bore/power encoded in the axis label. E.g.:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"indicating_range_maximum_value\",\n  \"axis\": \"meter_size (reduced bore, AC mains powered)\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"DN40, DN50, DN65\", \"value\": 9999999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThat's 4 attributes × 4 tables = 12 config_level entries. That's a lot but accurate. Alternatively, I could simplify since max is constant 9999999 within tables 9, 10, 11 — could just be a type_level? No, it varies across tables (page 12 rows have 99999999, 999999999 for full bore AC mains continuation; table 12 has variations too).\n\nI think emitting both matrix_tables (preserving the exact tables) and config_level entries is the right approach per rules 4/5. But maybe that's redundant. The R137 example does both: matrix_tables AND model_level. So doing both is consistent with the guidance.\n\nActually, for config_level, maybe I should flatten conditions to include all three dimensions. Like condition: \"reduced_bore + AC_mains @ DN40, DN50, DN65\". Hmm.\n\nLet me define the axis as \"bore_type × power_source × meter_size\" and each condition